# Suppressions for the release vulnerability gate (LOCI-044, §9.2).
#
# THE GATE IS ON FIXABLE CRITICAL AND HIGH, ANYWHERE IN THE IMAGE (🧭 3). It
# does not exempt the upstream PHP base: the largest CVE surface here is
# ImageMagick and its delegate stack, which LaraOCI installs itself (D13), and
# --ignore-unfixed already handles the "upstream has no fix yet" case. What
# remains is a fixable base CVE, and the control for that is §9.3's weekly
# rebuild against a current base - not an exemption.
#
# So an entry here is an admission that a release is shipping with a known,
# fixable CRITICAL or HIGH. That is sometimes the right call. It is never the
# right call indefinitely, which is why:
#
#   EVERY ENTRY CARRIES AN EXPIRY DATE, and the release notes list the ones
#   that were active for that release.
#
# Format (Trivy honours the `exp:` field):
#
#   CVE-2026-12345 exp:2026-09-01   # why, and what unblocks it
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Active entries. Each one is listed in the release notes of every release it
# is active for (LOCI-047), so a suppression cannot quietly outlive its reason.

# The gate's first real block, on the 🚦 Gate 2 staging run of 2026-08-11.
#
# Both are Linux KERNEL vulnerabilities (KVM, SCTP) reported against
# linux-libc-dev, which is the kernel HEADERS package - build-time C
# declarations, with no code path in a running container. The container uses the
# HOST's kernel, which these headers neither contain nor influence.
#
# Not suppressed as noise, though. linux-libc-dev is genuinely removable, and
# doing so would end this whole class permanently: it is reachable only through
# libc6-dev, so purging it takes g++, libstdc++-14-dev and the rest of the C++
# toolchain with it - a measured 87 MB off every one of the six images. That is
# deferred rather than rejected, because it changes §7.4's documented node-gyp
# trade, which tests/structure/builder.yaml pins. See D40.
#
# What unblocks these two specifically: Debian trixie publishing 6.12.101-1.
# The image already tracks 6.12.100-1 - the base ships 6.12.96-1 and the build's
# own apt-get update pulls it forward, which is what fixed the other twelve.
CVE-2026-64561 exp:2026-09-15   # linux-libc-dev 6.12.100-1, fixed in 6.12.101-1
CVE-2026-64564 exp:2026-09-15   # linux-libc-dev 6.12.100-1, fixed in 6.12.101-1
